The appointing authority, any authority it is subordinate to, the disciplinary authority, or another authority empowered by the President can suspend you [(Rule 10(1), PDF p. 8)](https://dopt.gov.in/sites/default/files/CCS-CCA-Rules-FINAL.pdf#page=8). The rule allows this in three situations [(Rule 10(1), PDF p. 8)](https://dopt.gov.in/sites/default/files/CCS-CCA-Rules-FINAL.pdf#page=8):

- a disciplinary proceeding against you is contemplated or pending;
- the authority believes you have engaged in activities prejudicial to the security of the State;
- a criminal case against you is under investigation, inquiry or trial.

If an authority lower than the appointing authority suspends you, it must report the circumstances to the appointing authority at once [(Rule 10(1), proviso, PDF p. 8)](https://dopt.gov.in/sites/default/files/CCS-CCA-Rules-FINAL.pdf#page=8). You can also be deemed suspended, for example after more than 48 hours in custody [(Rule 10(2), PDF p. 8)](https://dopt.gov.in/sites/default/files/CCS-CCA-Rules-FINAL.pdf#page=8).
